From 30970e8290b7e23185fe72bb77342bd52ebedf12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rocker14427 <Rocker14427@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 19:58:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 89/91] misc: mediatek: give the panel bias rails their MT6370 provider

The landed dispsys core calls two functions that had no provider in this tree:
display_bias_regulator_init() (ddp_drv.c:688) and disp_late_bias_enable() (:692), both reached from
the panel-on path, both declared in drivers/misc/mediatek/lcm/inc/lcm_pmic.h. Landing only
lcm_pmic.c, as an earlier 4.19-era port round did, turned those two references into no-ops rather
than into anything driving hardware: lcm_pmic.c selects between real regulator calls and stubs with

	#if defined(CONFIG_RT5081_PMU_DSV) || defined(CONFIG_MT6370_PMU_DSV)

at line 10, and in a tree with neither symbol it compiles the #else - a file of "return 0"
functions, 140 lines of the 149 dropped. This patch lands both sides of that #if and the branch
selection that makes them meet.

What is new, all of it copied verbatim from the 4.19.325 vendor tree:

  - drivers/misc/mediatek/pmic/mt6370/v1/: mt6370_pmu_{i2c,regmap,irq,subdevs,core}.c under
    CONFIG_MFD_MT6370_PMU and mt6370_pmu_{dsv,dsv_debugfs}.c under CONFIG_MT6370_PMU_DSV, with the
    four headers they include (mt6370_pmu.h, _core.h, _debugfs.h, _dsv_debugfs.h). v1/ and not
    drivers/misc/mediatek/pmic/mt6370/, because the vendor's own parent Makefile descends into v1/
    for CONFIG_MACH_MT6768=y (pmic/mt6370/Makefile:1-4): v1/ is the variant this SoC builds, and
    the file at the top level, which an earlier round measured as an 885-line dead variant, is not
    what this board ships.
  - drivers/misc/mediatek/include/mt-plat/rt-regmap.h (216 lines) and
    drivers/misc/mediatek/rt-regmap/rt-regmap.c (1,646 lines): inc/mt6370_pmu.h:9 includes that
    header, so the include stage needed it before anything else here could compile.
  - drivers/misc/mediatek/lcm/lcm_pmic.c (149 lines), keyed on CONFIG_MTK_DISP_BRINGUP, because it
    is display code whose caller is under that switch and this tree has no stock MTK_LCM symbol.

Nothing is adapted, and no symbol in this patch is invented: the only content the vendor did not
have is the five Makefiles/Kconfigs that wire it up (plus two source lines in drivers/misc/Kconfig
and the obj lines in drivers/misc/Makefile, which is where this tree already wires mediatek/m4u/
and mediatek/video/). The stock "-Wall -Werror" subdir flags are not carried; this tree builds
pre-existing vendor display code with warnings of its own.

Branch selection is done through Kconfig, not with a -D, and that choice is load-bearing twice:

  - CONFIG_MT6370_PMU_DSV is enabled where stock enables it - the board fragment now carries
    MFD_MT6370_PMU=y and MT6370_PMU_DSV=y, matching even_defconfig:1687-1693 - so the bias consumer
    reads the same switch the provider is built from. A -D on one file would have made the two
    disagree for anyone using menuconfig, and a def_bool on the display switch would have made a
    board PMIC depend on a display feature.
  - CONFIG_RT_REGMAP arrives through MFD_MT6370_PMU's stock "select RT_REGMAP". Without it,
    mt6370_pmu_regmap.c's whole body (#ifdef CONFIG_RT_REGMAP, line 12 to 370) vanishes,
    mt6370_pmu_regmap_register() returns 0 without setting chip->rd, and mt6370_pmu_core.c:166
    calls rt_regmap_cache_reload() with a NULL handle. A probe that builds, links and then oopses is
    exactly what a green compile hides; the census below is what caught it.

Two other #ifdef sites were checked rather than assumed. mt6370_pmu_i2c.c:177 reads
"!defined(CONFIG_MTK_GPIO) || defined(CONFIG_MTK_GPIOLIB_STAND)": neither symbol exists in this tree
and neither is set in even_defconfig, so stock and this port take the same of_get_named_gpio()
branch. DEBUG_FS is y here and is not set in even_defconfig, so mt6370_pmu_dsv_debugfs.c registers
/sys/kernel/debug/mtk_dsv (a 246-line debug-only divergence, no hardware access).

Measured in a build tree reset to this landing commit (tree 7320325c38fd188de726f3ba658d0f6b80e7eb6),
config of record = the board config apply.sh produces from upstream-port/dev/*.fragment (sha256
prefix 099cdd6421b6), whole tree, 2 CPUs. That first run of the gate was itself instructive: it
printed "0 undefined references" in both directions while rt-regmap.c was failing to compile, because
a build that dies in the compile stage never reaches the link, so the gate now asserts the "LD
vmlinux" marker and the object count, not just an error count - and rt-regmap/Makefile gained the
same two -I paths drivers/misc/mediatek/m4u/Makefile:11-13 carries, which is what the vendor's global
subdir-ccflags at drivers/misc/mediatek/Makefile:3 supplies in the BSP.

  - display switch OFF (the state every build must survive): rc=0, 0 compile errors, 0 undefined
    references, vmlinux 168,340,520 bytes, Image.gz-dtb 12,228,271 bytes. The growth over 0088
    (167,987,640 / 12,204,087) is the sub-PMIC, rt-regmap and mfd-core now being built for the
    board, and nm on that vmlinux finds mt6370_pmu_regmap_register and rt_regmap_device_register but
    zero hits for display_bias_regulator_init: the provider is in the image, the display consumer is
    not, which is exactly the split this patch intends.
  - display switch ON: rc=2 (the pre-existing gaps), 0 compile errors, 0 diagnostics of any kind in
    the nine new objects (2,449 lines of driver + 1,646 of rt-regmap.c + 149 of lcm_pmic.c against
    5.15's regmap, gpiolib and regulator headers), all 9 objects present (mt6370_pmu_regmap.o 153,768
    with the real CONFIG_RT_REGMAP body, rt-regmap.o 196,712, lcm_pmic.o 77,944).
  - the whole-tree link goes from 502 to 499 undefined-reference lines and from 80 to 78 distinct
    names; the closed pair is exactly disp_late_bias_enable and display_bias_regulator_init and the
    set of newly introduced names is empty.
  - nm census: the nine objects define 39 distinct global text symbols and none of them is defined
    anywhere else in the tree.
  - drivers/misc/mediatek/include/mt-plat/rt-regmap.h: the header the vendor keeps in
    include/mt-plat/, landed beside the aee.h this tree already carries, and the DTB is untouched:
    arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6768.dtb is byte-identical to 0088's (122,474 bytes, sha256
    prefix 34a7e6b536a3), because no DT edit was needed or made.

One thing this slice does not do, stated because it bounds the claim: the driver binds nothing yet.
The DTB this tree appends is CONFIG_BUILD_ARM64_APPENDED_DTB_IMAGE_NAMES="mediatek/mt6768", built
from arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6768.dts, and that file carries the MT6370 *configuration* node
(in the decompiled dtb: mt6370_pmu_dts with #interrupt-cells, mt6370,intr_gpio = <&pio 3 0> and the
legacy mt6370,intr_gpio_num = <3>, plus mt6370_dsvp / mt6370_dsvn with regulator-name "dsv_pos" and
"dsv_neg") but no I2C client under i2c5@11016000 - the client node, subpmic_pmu@34 with
compatible = "mediatek,subpmic_pmu" and reg = <0x34>, is in arch/arm64/boot/dts/oplus6768_20761/
cust.dtsi:151, a board file landed for parity that this tree does not compile (that directory holds
one file and no .dts). So the sub-PMIC probe will not run and the rails will not be driven until the
DT surface of record settles: the board node also brings the bus's own clock-frequency = <3400000>
and mediatek,use-push-pull, which the landed i2c5 node does not have, and the i2c5 node has no
#address-cells/#size-cells for a child to live in. Which side of that to change is an open
architectural question (grow the SoC dts, or make a board dts the port's DT of record), so it is not
decided by this patch. The vendor driver needs no edit for either answer: its of table already lists
both "mediatek,mt6370_pmu" and "mediatek,subpmic_pmu" (mt6370_pmu_i2c.c:342-347).

What this slice does establish is the link-level fact: the two panel-bias references from the landed
dispsys core are now satisfied by code that talks to the chip, not by lcm_pmic.c's #else stubs, and
both directions of the build - board config alone, and board config plus the display switch - are
measured.
